prs.body:
Fixes #64980

## Problem

With `experimentalDecorators` and define semantics (`useDefineForClassFields`, the default at target ES2022+), a decorated `abstract` property was emitted as a class field. Every instance then got an own `data: undefined` that shadowed whatever the decorator installed on the prototype. tsc 6.0.3 erased the field.

```ts
abstract class Base {
  @dec abstract data: string;
}
```

**Before:**

```js
class Base { data; }
// plus the __decorate call
```

**After:**

```js
class Base { }
// plus the __decorate call
```

## Cause

For decorated `declare`/`abstract` properties under `experimentalDecorators`, `typeeraser.go` keeps the node so `LegacyDecoratorsTransformer` can emit `__decorate`. It rebuilds the node with `VisitModifiers`, which elides `abstract`. `visitPropertyDeclaration` in `legacydecorators.go` then finds neither `NodeFlagsAmbient` nor `ModifierFlagsAbstract` and emits a field. `declare` is unaffected because the node keeps `NodeFlagsAmbient`.

## Fix

When preserving a decorated `abstract` property, append a `declare` modifier so the decorator transform erases the field. This matches Strada, which appends `ModifierFlags.Ambient` to the preserved node.

## Tests

Added `legacyDecoratorAbstractPropertyDefine` (target ES2022, `experimentalDecorators`, `useDefineForClassFields`). The baseline shows no field on `Base` and the `__decorate` call intact. Also checked manually that the repro prints `false` from prototype.

Extracted out of #65063

```ts
interface I {
  [key: string];
}
```

was previously a grammar error; now it's an implicit `any`.
